  "ms": "(a)\nKnowledge 2 Application 1 Analysis 1\nKnowledge and Analysis: Up to 3 marks for one reason e.g.\n• Maximum price is the ceiling price above which the price does not go (1)\n• Maximum price is set below the market equilibrium price (1)\n• Increases student affordability (1) causing an extension in demand / low-income students increase uptake (1)\n• Encourages consumption (1) internalising external benefits / third party effects (1)\n• Increase in consumer surplus (1) because the price of tuition has fallen (1)\n• Government may want to increase the proportion of young people at university (1) to increase future labour productivity (1) and therefore increase the external benefit/tax yield/revenue (1)\nApplication: Up to 1 mark for a relevant example e.g.\n• Maximum undergraduate tuition fees in England (4)\nare £9 250 (1)\n• Fees at the same level for 2 years (1)\nNB A diagram may be used to earn knowledge, application or analysis marks (2) NB Must be one reason\n\n(b)\nThe only correct answer is B A is incorrect as there would be an extension in demand C is incorrect as there would be excess demand D is incorrect as there would be a contraction in supply",
