  "section": "B",
  "text": "(d) Assess the benefits Kenya has gained from its membership of the EAC since 2000. (10)",
  "ms": "Knowledge 2, Application 2, Analysis 2\nPossible benefits include:\n• increased regional trade has led to faster economic\n2\ngrowth\n• firms can take advantage of increased economies of scale\n• trade creation\n• free movement of labour may fill labour shortages in individual countries\n• increased investment, either domestic or foreign\n• negotiating together may give the countries more power at, e.g., WTO talks. (6)\nEvaluation 4\nPossible costs include:\n• trade diversion\n• loss of revenue from import tariffs from fellow members.\nGeneral evaluative points:\n• Kenya is the largest economy in the EAC, so has the smallest additional economies of scale to gain\n• 'Kenya’s exports to the other EAC members were about US$1.2 billion in 2010' (Extract 2), so benefits appear to be significant\n• Kenya probably gains less benefit from the smaller countries being co-members of the EAC, e.g. Burundi.",
